{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'pwd; ls -la; for f in strategy_validation_feedback.json strategy_validation_feedback.md VENUE.md; do if [ -f \"$f\" ]; then printf '\"'\\\\n--- %s ---\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; cat \"$f\"; fi; done; printf '\"'\\\\n--- skills ---\\\\n'; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"/workspace/paper\ntotal 4092\ndrwx------ 5 agent agent    4096 Nov  7 03:45 .\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent    4096 Nov  7 03:30 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     747 Nov  7 03:30 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent    4096 Nov  7 03:30 .codex\nprw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov  7 03:45 .paper_strategy_repair_round_1_stream_20261107_033010.fifo\n-rw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov  7 03:30 .paper_two_stage_enabled\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    3685 Nov  7 03:45 VENUE.md\ndrwx------ 2 agent agent    4096 Nov  7 03:30 figures\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      16 Nov  7 03:30 global_memory -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   13792 Nov  7 03:30 global_memory_recent.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    5897 Nov  7 03:30 global_memory_summary.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    8107 Nov  7 03:45 memory.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   26973 Nov  2 14:28 paper.bst\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    8934 Nov  2 14:28 paper.sty\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 2042115 Nov  7 03:30 paper_data.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent    4096 Nov  7 03:34 paper_diagnostics\n-rw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov  7 03:30 paper_diagnostics_20261107_033010_107220.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    4134 Nov  7 03:45 paper_events_20261107_033010.jsonl\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     791 Nov  7 03:30 paper_prep_20261107_033010.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   29813 Nov  7 03:44 paper_strategy.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   22432 Nov  7 03:44 paper_strategy.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent 1519028 Nov  7 03:45 paper_strategy_agent_codex_20261107_033010.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   46907 Nov  7 03:36 paper_strategy_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov  7 03:45 paper_strategy_repair_agent_codex_20261107_033010_round_1.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   51248 Nov  7 03:45 paper_strategy_repair_prompt_round_1.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    9418 Nov  7 03:33 reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   10462 Nov  7 03:33 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  233431 Nov  7 03:34 reference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261107_033010.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   31360 Nov  7 03:30 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     265 Nov  7 03:34 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     279 Nov  7 03:34 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    3509 Nov  7 03:36 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    7212 Nov  7 03:36 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   28682 Nov  7 03:36 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      16 Nov  7 03:30 results -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     373 Nov  7 03:45 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     388 Nov  7 03:45 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent       7 Nov  7 03:30 task -> ../task\n\n--- strategy_validation_feedback.json ---\n{\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-07T03:45:29.397395+00:00\",\n  \"hard_errors\": [\n    \"paper_strategy.json supplementary_plan.planned_subsections[5].source_ids must be a non-empty list\"\n  ],\n  \"ok\": false,\n  \"paper_dir\": \"work/results/t09/codex/20261107_014043/paper\"\n}\n--- strategy_validation_feedback.md ---\n# Paper strategy validation feedback\n\n- generated_at: 2026-11-07T03:45:29.397395+00:00\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t09/codex/20261107_014043/paper`\n- ok: `False`\n\n## Hard errors\n- paper_strategy.json supplementary_plan.planned_subsections[5].source_ids must be a non-empty list\n\n--- VENUE.md ---\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n\n--- skills ---\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat VENUE.md; printf '\\\\n--- instructions ---\\\\n'; find .. -name AGENTS.md -print; printf '\\\\n--- strategy ---\\\\n'; cat paper_strategy.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat VENUE.md; printf '\\\\n--- instructions ---\\\\n'; find .. -name AGENTS.md -print; printf '\\\\n--- strategy ---\\\\n'; cat paper_strategy.json\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n\n--- instructions ---\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/vercel/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/data-analytics/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/zoom/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/prototype/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/mobile-app/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_014045_1794015645066453399/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/vercel/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_014045_1794015645066453399/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_014045_1794015645066453399/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_014045_1794015645066453399/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/data-analytics/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_014045_1794015645066453399/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/zoom/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_014045_1794015645066453399/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/prototype/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_014045_1794015645066453399/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/mobile-app/AGENTS.md\n\n--- strategy ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Endpoint-Tuned Regression under Network Interference: Accuracy and Limits\",\n  \"central_question\": \"How accurately does endpoint-risk-tuned one-hop regression estimate the global average treatment effect across the evaluated networks, interference mechanisms, and assignment designs, relative to simple regression baselines?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"In the evaluated synthetic-outcome settings, endpoint-tuned exposure regression reduces error relative to assignment-only comparators in many spillover conditions but performs worse in aggregate than linear one-hop OLS, while near-noise-level in-sample residuals coexist with substantial endpoint errors under threshold and omitted two-hop interference.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The strongest evidence is the completed final evaluation, not the development score: final.evidence.episodes contains 24 seed blocks, each with four graph families and six scientific conditions, permitting direct matched comparisons of the frozen estimator with recorded baselines. The scientifically useful result is conditional accuracy and failure, including an unfavorable aggregate comparison with one-hop OLS. The four graph families belong to one shared question, with family-specific evidence rather than separate chronological experiments. Endpoint-targeted tuning is a concrete implemented method, but neither its isolated benefit nor the cause of every error is established. A universal-improvement story, a broad architecture comparison, and a search-progression story are weaker because component ablations and valid final results for those alternative families are absent.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"recentered\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"This is a fresh framing, not a manuscript-edit run: no resolved edit-context files, previous_paper, aggregated, extra_tasks, or contributions scaffold is present. Recentered denotes moving from the source task's broad accuracy agenda to the supported endpoint-regression comparison and its limitations; it does not assert that a prior manuscript was revised. Preserving a hypothetical prior story would invent evidence. Broadening to a shared estimator/architecture family is unsupported. Centering the completed final comparison is preferable to either broadening or a development-improvement narrative.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"The inspected final-evaluated method's available source is results/ideas/03_endpoint_contrast_sure_for_exposure_regression/program_1/src/program.py, linked by paper_data.best_correct and results/best_program_manifest.json. It implements quadratic one-hop exposure features, endpoint-risk ridge selection, estimability checks, and fixed-hinge fallback; it is not a neural or implemented two-hop estimator. The final manifest records a frozen program-tree hash, but this strategy does not claim an independent file-by-file hash verification.\",\n    \"The original implementation at task/program_0/src/program.py uses fixed one-hop hinges and fixed ridge regularization. Its development evidence is not a matched final comparison with the frozen estimator. Omit an original-versus-selected final contrast rather than mixing search and final results; the directly recorded final one-hop OLS comparator remains essential.\",\n    \"paper_data.final_evaluation was read independently: final.qualification.status is completed, final.qualification.outcome is qualified, and final.qualification.correct is true. These reported checks do not establish superiority, model adequacy, generalization, or publication readiness. No downstream_study or study.* grounding sources exist in this bundle.\",\n    \"The final records, including Stanford3 and the unfavorable comparisons, change the center of gravity from optimizing a development score to mapping conditional endpoint error. The empty figures directory and absent ablation_studies require new visualizations of existing records, not invented experiments.\",\n    \"The two-hop GCV branch has a failed evaluation artifact and no usable performance result. Its stderr reports a disconnection before receiving candidate responses; the underlying cause and estimator performance remain unknown. It is missing evidence, not a measured loss or evidence against two-hop adjustment. No externally staged new/old task families or prior manuscript were supplied.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Endpoint-risk-tuned quadratic one-hop regression\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The frozen final-evaluated method: covariates, own treatment, neighbor treatment fraction, treatment-exposure interaction, exposure curvature, and treatment-curvature interaction; centered/scaled SVD ridge with a fixed penalty grid and endpoint-contrast risk criterion. Explain guards, constant-outcome bypass, and fixed-hinge fallback without claiming their observed frequencies.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Difference in means\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"A recorded final comparator that ignores interference and provides an interpretable reference for direct-treatment contrasts versus the global intervention target. Retain its favorable no-spillover results.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Own-treatment OLS with covariates\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"A recorded final comparator separating ordinary covariate adjustment from explicit exposure adjustment. Its similarity to difference in means is informative rather than grounds for silently dropping it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Linear one-hop OLS with covariates\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The strongest recorded aggregate final comparator and the central negative control against a universal tuning-benefit narrative. It adds neighbor treatment fraction but not the proposed curvature features or tuning rule; it is not a same-feature tuning ablation.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Lead with an absolute-error table for all four methods: equal-weight mean of the 24 family-by-condition RMSEs and mean absolute cell bias, with seed-block uncertainty computed from existing final records. The selected method's recorded mean capped normalized RMSE is 0.6140475146408374 and mean absolute capped normalized bias is 0.4597691498768297. Check clipping explicitly before equating these with uncapped quantities.\",\n    \"Preserve the unfavorable aggregate baseline comparison in the main text. Descriptive reaggregation of final.evidence.episodes gives equal-cell mean uncapped RMSE 0.4598497552813962 for one-hop OLS and 1.36760394415568 for difference in means, versus 0.6140475146408373 for the selected estimator. These are deterministic summaries of existing observations, not newly run experiments, confidence intervals, or proof of statistical significance; the writer must materialize their derivation and grounding before reporting rounded values.\",\n    \"Show the complete four-family by six-condition error matrix through matched RMSE ratios to each of the three baselines, backed by absolute errors in the table and supplement. Retain no-spillover regressions, threshold failures, and omitted two-hop failures as central evidence rather than highlighting only favorable strong-spillover comparisons.\",\n    \"Use candidate observed_fit_rmse versus absolute uncapped GATE error to illustrate why in-sample fit is not an endpoint-accuracy certificate. Recorded fit residuals are not test prediction error, and no baseline observed-fit measurements or predictive-GCV comparison are available.\",\n    \"Compare the strong linear spillover condition with its cluster-assignment counterpart within graph families using the same seed blocks. This is a bounded design comparison, not evidence that graph clustering is optimal or that support alone causes the error change. Treat minimum/maximum exposure and endpoint fractions as descriptive supporting measurements, not identification tests.\",\n    \"Separate scientific performance from evaluation integrity. Anchor the reported final completion and qualification only in final.qualification.*; do not display the composite utility as GATE accuracy. Development results, unscored branches, and search counts do not enter main comparisons. No study.* sources exist to support reporting campaign selection statistics or search score differences.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Figure 1, early on page 1 or 2: a conceptual overview of observed network experiment, one-hop exposure regression with contrast-targeted tuning, and the all-treated minus all-control prediction target, explicitly separating observed fitting from counterfactual endpoint prediction.\",\n    \"Figure 2: four matched-comparison panels, one per graph family, showing the selected estimator's RMSE divided by each recorded baseline's RMSE across all six conditions; pair with a main-text absolute-error table so relative plots cannot conceal poor absolute performance.\",\n    \"Figure 3: a two-panel diagnostic composite juxtaposing in-sample fit with endpoint error and the matched individual-versus-cluster assignment comparison for strong linear spillover. Show failures and uncertainty, not search trajectories.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"endpoint_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Make the global intervention target and the distinction between observed fitting and endpoint prediction legible at a glance, without asserting empirical calibration of the tuning rule.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"observed inputs, fitted regression, endpoint contrast\",\n      \"metric\": \"none; conceptual relationships only\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 0.98,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"The inspected paper.sty is single-column, with 5.5in text width and 9in text height; column width equals full text width, not a 2.6in double-column slot. Target 5.39in width by about 1.7-1.9in height, with caption occupying about one quarter page in total. Place on page 1 or 2. Use one cohesive panel with at most three horizontal zones, at most six short conceptual labels, no tiny equations, and rendered text at least 8.5pt. All-treated/all-control are prediction inputs, not observed outcome labels. Use an editable draw.io source, not invented empirical curves; the task's graph sketch is explicitly schematic.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"conditional_accuracy\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Expose both improvements over assignment-only baselines and regressions relative to one-hop OLS in every final graph-by-condition cell, using final.evidence.episodes only.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"faceted_dot_whisker\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"baseline comparator; selected estimator is the common numerator\",\n      \"metric\": \"cell RMSE(selected) / cell RMSE(comparator), computed from uncapped final errors; below one favors the selected estimator\",\n      \"split_by\": \"graph family, with six condition rows in each panel\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use a 2-by-2 grid over the inspected 5.5in single text column, about 3.8-4.2in high plus a concise caption, roughly half a body page. Each approximately 2.6in-wide panel has six horizontal condition rows and three dodged comparator markers, a shared logarithmic ratio axis, and a vertical reference at one; use one shared three-entry legend. Labels such as No spill, Weak linear, Strong linear, Threshold, Two-hop, and Cluster are defined in the protocol table. Render labels at least 8.5pt, markers clearly separated, and at most two panels per row. Compute ratios of per-cell RMSEs, not means of per-seed ratios. Seed-block bootstrap intervals are planned summaries, not currently supplied estimates; do not attach significance stars. Report absolute errors beside this figure in a main-text table.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 4,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"diagnostic_limits\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show that near-noise-level in-sample residuals can coexist with endpoint error, and summarize the bounded assignment-design contrast without causal attribution to support or tuning.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter_and_paired_dot_whisker\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"panel A: threshold, two-hop, and remaining conditions; panel B: assignment design\",\n      \"metric\": \"panel A: observed-fit RMSE versus absolute uncapped GATE error; panel B: selected-estimator RMSE under strong linear spillover with individual versus cluster assignment\",\n      \"split_by\": \"two diagnostic questions; panel B has four graph-family categories\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use two panels in one row across the inspected 5.5in single-column text width, approximately 2.4-2.7in high plus caption, about one third page. Panel A uses transparent points and three semantic groups, not six colored mechanism series; show all scientific records and no fitted causal trend. Its observations are seed-dependent and are not independent sample-size evidence. Panel B has four graph categories and two design series with seed-block uncertainty, not four overlapping method curves. Keep labels at least 8.5pt and use the same condition names as Figure 2. If crowding occurs, stack the two panels and revise the page allocation rather than shrinking text; all support-fraction tables remain in the supplement.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 4,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n    }\n  ],\n  \"supplementary_plan\": {\n    \"purpose\": \"After the references, provide the complete recorded conditional evidence, implementation specification, and reproducibility/audit boundaries needed to inspect the main thesis; do not use the supplement to hide the unfavorable one-hop OLS comparison or the principal threshold/two-hop failures.\",\n    \"planned_subsections\": [\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Complete Conditional Error Tables\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Tabulate signed bias, absolute bias, uncapped RMSE, and replicate counts for all four methods in every graph-by-condition cell, using validated true_gate, contrast, and baseline_estimates. Preserve pairing and distinguish mean cell RMSE from pooled RMSE. Include uncertainty derived from the existing seed records and describe its resampling unit. These are reanalyses, not new simulation runs.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.mean_capped_normalized_rmse\", \"final.metric.mean_absolute_capped_normalized_bias\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Exposure Support and Residual Diagnostics\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Provide existing exposure minima/maxima, fractions at zero and one, linear-design condition measurements, and observed-fit residual summaries by family and condition. Relate them descriptively to endpoint errors and individual/cluster assignment. The condition measurement is for the evaluator's simple linear design, not the selected quadratic reference; none of these fields certifies endpoint identifiability. No new calibration or coverage experiment is implied.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Estimator Specification and Conditional Risk Criterion\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Document the available selected source at results/ideas/03_endpoint_contrast_sure_for_exposure_regression/program_1/src/program.py: feature map, saved centering/scaling, SVD rank tolerance, contrast row-space tolerance, residual degrees of freedom, the n times {0, 1e-6, 1e-4, 1e-2, 1} penalty grid, deterministic minimum selection, constant bypass, and fixed-hinge fallback. The result source links to the selected source directory; final source IDs identify its evaluated evidence, not an independently verified implementation hash. Distinguish a fixed-penalty risk identity under a correct reference and iid homoskedastic Gaussian noise from the optimistic selected minimum. No selected-penalty distribution, fallback frequency, or empirical risk calibration is recorded.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Benchmark Construction and Reproduction\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Use task/resources/final/manifest.json, task/task_contract.json, task/requirements.txt, and task/evaluate_final.py as local protocol provenance alongside the final records. Specify graph generation, all six outcome/design conditions, covariates, unit Gaussian noise, the deterministic BFS-cluster design, preserved isolates, and length-two walks with backtracking. Separate synthetic topology replication from repeated assignments/outcomes on the fixed Stanford3 graph. Record seeds, checksums, declared dependency versions, recorded runtime limits versus measured elapsed time, and exact reproduction commands only after checking available entrypoints. Map each eventual table/figure to a source script; those plot scripts do not exist yet. Explain capped metrics and equal-cell aggregation, and retain required graph attribution and share-alike caveats.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Evaluation Integrity and Missing Evidence\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Report completed final evaluation and its reported qualification from final.qualification.* separately from statistical accuracy, and summarize constant-outcome, relabeling, and replay checks only to their documented scope. Candidate internal diagnostics are marked unverified. State that original fixed-hinge development evidence is not a matched final comparator, component ablations are absent, and broader stress studies were not performed. A brief nonchronological missing-evidence note may cite the local two-hop branch's evaluation_failure.json and harness_evaluate_stderr.log: disconnection is observed, underlying cause and performance unknown. Do not report campaign counts, winner margins, or search deltas without study.* grounding; do not reinterpret a missing score as a loss.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.correct\", \"final.metric.control_success_fraction\", \"final.metric.failed_episode_fraction\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"LLM Usage\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Include the venue-required separate section disclosing documented LLM roles in ideation, candidate-code generation, reference-query planning, framing, writing, and figure preparation as applicable. Use actual process artifacts under results/ideas and the local strategy/agent records; distinguish work already performed from future writing/figure activity. These process artifacts, not scientific metric source IDs, ground the disclosure. Include no author identities, personal links, funding, or acknowledgements.\",\n        \"source_ids\": []\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"forbidden_headings\": [\n    \"Search Progression\",\n    \"Campaign Phases\",\n    \"Milestones\",\n    \"Timeline of Improvements\",\n    \"First Successful Estimator\",\n    \"Experiment A / Experiment B\",\n    \"From Baseline to Winner\",\n    \"Architecture Sweep\",\n    \"Ablation Results (when no component ablations exist)\"\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_phrases\": [\n    \"we then tried\",\n    \"first valid result\",\n    \"subsequent improvements\",\n    \"next-best\",\n    \"later variants\",\n    \"search winner proves superiority\",\n    \"universally outperforms\",\n    \"state of the art\",\n    \"robust to arbitrary interference\",\n    \"validated endpoint-risk calibration\",\n    \"unbiased after tuning\",\n    \"predictive GCV is inferior\",\n    \"the two-hop method failed scientifically\",\n    \"eligible\",\n    \"correct and eligible\",\n    \"correctness rate\",\n    \"combined_score\",\n    \"worker_01\",\n    \"worker_02\",\n    \"rubric\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_writer\": [\n    \"Follow write-paper-standalone only at the later drafting stage. This strategy is ready for a bounded comparison paper, not a claim of publication acceptance, novel causal identification, or final-deliverable completeness. Do not draft paper.tex, claims_grounding.json, or run final paper validation during strategy generation.\",\n    \"ICLR 2026 is fixed by VENUE.md: anonymized conference submission, 8-9 body pages excluding references; an eight-page body must finish at least 75% full. No names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, self-identifying repositories, or internal URLs. Include a separate LLM Usage section, preferably after references. The supplied paper.sty confirms single-column 5.5in by 9in geometry; its historical header does not independently verify official 2026 template provenance. Check official-template provenance during final preparation and regenerate figures if geometry differs, rather than silently treating this as a double-column format.\",\n    \"Target approximately 8.75 substantive body pages including figures and tables: title/abstract 0.40; introduction and early overview 1.10; related work 0.65; target and interference setup 0.90; endpoint-tuned estimator 1.50; empirical protocol and condition table 0.95; conditional comparisons and diagnostic figures 2.15; discussion/limitations 0.85; conclusion 0.25. These are layout allocations, not measured page counts. Compress repetition rather than exceeding nine pages; spend spare space explaining the risk formula, baseline definitions, aggregation, uncertainty, and failure cases, never diary or filler.\",\n    \"Use thesis-first headings such as Global Intervention Target, Endpoint-Tuned Exposure Regression, Empirical Protocol, Conditional Accuracy and Failure, and Scope and Limitations. Present all networks as condition-specific evidence under one question. The introduction's contributions are an implemented target-aware regression construction, a matched conditional comparison, and an explicit accuracy/fit limitation map; do not claim isolated benefits of curvature features or risk tuning.\",\n    \"Define GATE as the finite-population average difference between all-treated and all-control potential outcomes. The fitted estimate averages node-aligned predictions at those two assignments. Define neighbor exposure with isolates assigned zero; all-treated exposure need not equal one for isolates. Describe two-hop interference as length-two random-walk exposure including backtracking, not unique-distance-two exposure or the failed branch's nonbacktracking features.\",\n    \"Map condition IDs to scientific names using the final manifest: no spillover; weak linear neighbor spillover; strong linear neighbor spillover; threshold-plus-treatment interaction; two-hop spillover; and strong linear spillover under deterministic graph-BFS cluster assignment. The last condition changes assignment, not the outcome mechanism relative to strong linear spillover. All outcomes are synthetic, including on Stanford3; no real platform outcome or social causal effect is measured.\",\n    \"Explain the implemented endpoint-risk score as (a_lambda^T y_centered - a_0^T y_centered)^2 + 2*sigma_hat^2*a_lambda^T*a_0 - sigma_hat^2*||a_0||^2, where a_0 represents the estimable reference contrast and a_lambda the fixed-penalty contrast. State the correct-reference and iid homoskedastic Gaussian-noise assumptions, the row-space and degrees-of-freedom guards, and the lack of an unbiased post-selection interpretation. These are implementation/conditional analytical facts, not evidence that the assumptions hold in every condition or a new established theorem.\",\n    \"No same-feature fixed-penalty, OLS, or predictive-GCV ablations have usable final evidence. The proposed estimator jointly changes feature representation and regularization relative to the original fixed-hinge regression; comparisons with simple one-hop OLS likewise change more than one component. Do not attribute any improvement or regression solely to tuning, curvature, support, conditioning, or the fallback. Do not turn algebraic estimability into positivity, identification, or generalization.\",\n    \"Uncertainty must be recomputed from the 24 existing seed-level episode records, preserving every graph/condition/method observation within a resampled seed block. For ratios, recompute both methods' RMSEs in each resample before dividing; for the table, resample blocks before equal-cell averaging. A planned 95% paired seed-block percentile bootstrap with a fixed documented random seed may be used; specify resample count when implemented. No intervals have yet been produced. These quantify Monte Carlo variation in this fixed benchmark, not unit-level causal confidence-interval coverage or independent graph-population generalization. Per-condition comparisons are descriptive/exploratory and multiplicity is not resolved by the bootstrap.\",\n    \"Ground every scientific result view in final.evidence.episodes and the appropriate final.metric.* fields. The final qualification facts are completed, qualified, and true under final.qualification.status/outcome/correct, independently of downstream study metadata. Gate diagnostics not provided remain unknown; infer no validity/adaptivity failure or guarantee. If authoritative later evidence reports not_qualified, retain that outcome and its stated limitations in writing and review; do not infer its cause from retained metrics or erase it on publication grounds.\",\n    \"PRIVATE-ONLY provenance: paper_data.best_correct, result.best_correct_score, and results/best_program_manifest.json locate the available selected source; the final manifest supplies the frozen program-tree identifier. downstream_study and study.* are absent. Do not invent study.* IDs, expose selection-score internals in visible prose, report scored/unscored attempt counts, or subtract search and final scores. Do not silently treat the development-only original fixed-hinge method as a recorded final baseline. Missing two-hop performance does not justify eliminating two-hop adjustment as a scientific possibility.\",\n    \"Keep copy-observed and constant-prediction checks out of the substantive four-method comparison; they are degenerate behavioral controls, not credible scientific alternatives. Report task integrity checks briefly and do not let a high composite utility stand in for effect-estimation accuracy. Do not invent inference coverage, runtimes per estimator, selected-penalty histograms, contrast-weight norms, support probabilities, or fallback rates from fields that do not exist.\",\n    \"Required figure budgets are explicit: overview one panel; accuracy four panels arranged two per row; diagnostics two panels in one row. Never exceed three panels per row, six categories per panel, or four series per panel; the tighter figure-specific limits govern. All figures target the single full text column. Pair the quantitative comparison with a compact main table of absolute RMSE and absolute bias for all four methods, and a protocol table defining conditions and graph sizes. Do not substitute a favorable-cell ranking for the complete condition matrix.\",\n    \"Vector-first output: quantitative plotting scripts must export both PDF and 300-DPI PNG from the same source; LaTeX must use the PDF whenever it exists. The overview uses generate-concept-figure exactly once and preserves editable draw.io source; request a vector PDF export if available, otherwise regenerate raster output at least 2400px wide for this full-width placement. Any column-width raster in a changed layout must be at least 1200px wide. Never upscale existing rasters. If the concept workflow is impossible or fails, log the concrete reason before any documented vector fallback; do not silently replace it with an improvised schematic.\",\n    \"References are not an experiment. references.bib currently contains permitted starter-title matches Chin2018RegressionAF and Han2023ModelbasedRA, but references_provenance.json records retrieval metadata, not verified reading, and other returned statistical titles are not automatically relevant foundations for this criterion. Cite only existing keys after checking and reading permitted sources. Do not pad related work with unrelated retrieved hits, rely on questionable metadata years without checking, or invent missing graph-attribution citation keys. Any additional acquisition belongs to an authorized reference step, not to claims grounded in this strategy.\",\n    \"Respect the restricted-literature boundary: do not identify the prohibited paper, expand citation graphs, or read related-paper/reproduction/discussion pages. Allowed starter-source checks and graph provenance must not become unrestricted searches. If restricted material is encountered, stop and record where and how much was seen. No external research or new scientific runs were performed during this framing stage.\",\n    \"The supplement is a minimum grounded scope, not an authoritative posthoc ranking. Route later appendix-ranked analyses there without retroactively changing this strategy, and keep evidence essential to the central thesis in the main paper. Proposed missing studies, such as matched tuning ablations, noise stress tests, hidden endpoint twins, and inference calibration, belong in limitations/future work and must never be written as completed results.\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
